{"answer":"The lake stock is calculated by a community‑appointed lake monitor (or volunteer) who takes the lake’s measured stock at the beginning of each month. The community then sums all the individual planned quotas that the fishers record in the communal ledger to obtain the ‘planned total harvest’. If that planned total exceeds the allowed percentage of the lake stock, we apply a proportional reduction to every fisher’s quota.  The reduction factor is calculated as:  

    reduction_factor = (allowed_percentage × lake_stock) / planned_total   

Each fisher multiplies their individual quota by this factor, and the result becomes their new, adjusted quota for the month.  This ensures that the community’s harvest never exceeds the set cap while keeping each fisher’s share fair and proportional to their original plan.","revised_policy":"Limit each fisher to a maximum of 5 kg per trip and restrict the community’s total monthly harvest to no more than 10 % of the current lake stock.","revised_operationalization":"Lake stock is calculated by the lake monitor at the start of each month using the last measurement.  Each fisher records their catch in the communal ledger on every trip.  At the beginning of the month the community sums the declared planned quotas.  If the sum exceeds 10 % of the lake stock, every fisher’s quota is reduced by the same proportion (reduction_factor = (10 % × lake_stock) ÷ planned_total).  Any fisher who takes more than 5 kg or whose contribution pushes the community harvest over the cap must return the excess weight and pay a fine; fines are pooled for restoration and any surplus redistributed equally.","reasoning":"I kept the original 10 % cap to stay consistent with the proposal that won.  The explanation clarifies the roles and calculations so everyone understands how the rule is enforced and how quota reductions are applied proportionally."}

{"answer":"When a fisher’s monthly total hits 200 kg, the steward tallies the exact overage in the ledger at the monthly meeting. The 10 % penalty is calculated on that overage and the fisher pays it directly to the steward in cash or by exchanging a small amount of gear or supplies – whatever the community agrees on as a liquid value. The steward records the payment in the ledger as a ‘penalty received’ entry. All penalties collected in a month are pooled into the communal fund. At the end of the meeting, the steward counts the fund’s total and distributes it to approved projects for new nets, boat repairs, or conservation work, as decided by the group. Thus the penalty is collected in person at the meeting and the money is immediately earmarked for shared benefits.

I see no change needed to the wording of my proposal or its operationalisation.
","revised_policy":"Each fisher may take a maximum of 8 kg per trip and no more than 200 kg per month in total across all trips. Every trip, each fisher records their catch in a shared ledger that is reviewed at the monthly community meeting; if a fisher exceeds the 8 kg per‑trip limit, the excess must be returned to the lake; if a fisher exceeds the 200 kg monthly total, the community imposes a penalty equal to 10 % of the overage, which is redistributed as a communal fund for gear and conservation; all fishers must keep a personal reserve of at least 1 kg—any catch below that is considered waste and must be returned to the lake. At the end of each month, a rotating steward—chosen by the community—reviews all ledger entries, verifies weights, and corrects any discrepancies. The steward finalizes the communal fund allocation and ensures every fisher’s personal reserve requirement is met. All corrections are announced and documented at the monthly community meeting.","revised_operationalization":"1. Each fisher weighs their catch on a shared balance scale before departure and records the weight in the shared ledger. 2. The ledger is updated in real time and kept accessible to all fishers. 3. The steward randomly samples scales each month, verifies readings against ledger entries, and performs spot checks with a calibrated weigh‑bridge if needed. 4. If a discrepancy is found, the fisher must return the excess to the lake, pay the 10 % penalty on the overage, and the adjustment is logged in the ledger. 5. All corrections are announced at the monthly community meeting, and the steward records the final communal fund allocation for gear and conservation.","reasoning":"I answered the question by describing the practical payment method (cash or goods) and the pooling/allocating process, then confirmed the policy text and procedures remain unchanged."}

