{"answer":"The council is made up of the five fishers who are most active in the community, including myself, who agree to serve in a rotating fashion. Every Friday a single member is chosen by a random draw from the pool of fishers who have not sat on the council in the past month. Once a member has served a full four‑week cycle they sit out the next month and are again eligible to be drawn. This keeps the council’s composition fresh, gives everyone a fair chance to help enforce the rules, and ensures we can audit the logs consistently.

The council members must have no conflict of interest – they cannot be the one logging the catch for the week they sit on the council, and they are expected to be present in person for the audit. If a member is absent, the next person on the rotation steps in.

This structure meets the community’s need for accountability and avoids giving any single fisher too much influence over the enforcement process.","revised_policy":"Each fisher may take up to 12% of the lake's current stock per trip, with an absolute maximum of 15kg per trip, and no single fisher may cumulatively exceed 20% of the lake's stock in a calendar month. Before each trip, fishers submit a brief log of intended catch. A rotating council of community members reviews the logs weekly, confirms totals do not exceed the 12%/15kg limit, and publicly announces compliance. If a fisher exceeds the limit, the excess weight is returned to the lake and the fisher pays a small fine of $5 per kilogram of excess, which is added to a community maintenance fund. Logs are collected and stored in a shared ledger. The council meets every Friday to audit the logs against the 12%/15kg rule. Any deviation triggers automatic calculation of excess weight; the fisher is notified of the fine amount ($5 per kg) and must remit payment within 48 hours. The collected fines are deposited into the community maintenance fund.","revised_operationalization":"Fishers log intended catch each trip and record the actual weight caught in a shared ledger. The rotating council reviews the ledger weekly, summing each fisher’s catch to that date. They compare the monthly cumulative total for each fisher against 20% of the lake’s stock at the start of the month (or the stock at the time of the trip if that’s the agreed metric). If a fisher’s cumulative haul surpasses the 20% cap, the council automatically calculates the excess weight, notifies the fisher, and enforces a $5 per kg fine payable within 48 hours. The excess weight is returned to the lake. All fines are deposited into the community maintenance fund.","reasoning":"I described the council’s composition and rotation method to ensure fairness and accountability while preserving the original policy text."}

