{"answer":"When the lake’s biomass first falls below the 50 kg release threshold, the community meets to vote on whether to pull from the reserve.  If the vote is to release, the moment the reserve is emptied starts the 6‑month replenishment clock.  Every month the ledger records the lake’s biomass and a calendar entry notes the date the reserve was last used.  The reserve remains locked until the lake’s biomass has stayed below 50 kg and the six‑month counter has completed, at which point a new vote can be held to release it again.  This way we guarantee a full six‑month window for the lake to rebuild before we draw on the reserve again.

Restated policy (unchanged): Each trip may yield up to 4 kg, with 1 kg for personal sustenance and 3 kg deposited into the communal reserve.  The reserve is released only when the lake’s biomass falls below 70 kg, after a 6‑month replenishment period, and each fisher’s monthly total cannot exceed 8 % of the current lake biomass.

Operationalization: 1) The communal ledger logs every trip: catch weight, reserve deposit, and date. 2) Monthly meetings record the lake’s biomass. 3) When biomass < 70 kg, a vote is called.  4) If the reserve is released, the ledger stamps the release date and sets a 6‑month countdown (using the community calendar).  5) The reserve remains locked until the countdown expires and the lake’s biomass has risen above 70 kg; then a vote can be held to release again.  6) Monthly compliance checks compare each fisher’s cumulative catch to the 8 % limit, returning any excess to the reserve.","revised_policy":"Each trip may yield up to 4 kg, with 1 kg for personal sustenance and 3 kg deposited into the communal reserve.  The reserve is released only when the lake’s biomass falls below 70 kg, after a 6‑month replenishment period, and each fisher’s monthly total cannot exceed 8 % of the current lake biomass.","revised_operationalization":"1) Every trip is logged on the shared ledger with date, catch weight, reserve deposit, and personal sustenance. 2) The lake’s biomass is measured and recorded monthly.  3) When the biomass drops below 70 kg, a community vote is held to decide on releasing reserve funds.  4) If the reserve is released, the ledger records the release date and a 6‑month counter is started.  5) The reserve remains locked until the 6‑month period has elapsed and the lake’s biomass has recovered above 70 kg; at that point a new vote can be held to release again.  6) Monthly summaries compare each fisher’s cumulative catch to the 8 % limit; any excess is returned to the reserve, and violations are tracked for potential bans.","reasoning":"I clarified that the 6‑month replenishment period starts at the moment the reserve is released, not at the first dip below the threshold.  Tracking is done via the ledger’s release date and a calendar countdown, ensuring the lake has ample time to recover before the reserve can be used again.  The policy and operationalization remain unchanged from my original proposal."}

