{"answer":"Hey everyone, I reckon the best way to keep this fair is to have the monitor chosen by the fishers themselves. At the start of each month we’ll hold a quick secret ballot – anyone who is part of the fishing community can vote. Whoever gets the majority becomes the monitor for that month. If there’s a tie, we’ll do a simple draw of lots from a hat on the board. The chosen monitor will write down their own catch too, so they’re part of the record and can’t hide a mistake. They’ll tally the monthly totals, redistribute any excess, and enforce the ban on over‑catchers the next week. If anyone feels the monitor is acting unfairly, we can hold another vote to change the role for the next month. That way, the role stays transparent, accountable, and rotates among us.

We’ll keep the original rule about the 3 kg per trip, 1 kg personal keep, and 20 % monthly cap. The only addition is this simple, community‑run selection process for the monitor.

That’s all, and I’m happy to fish with the rest of you and keep the lake healthy!","revised_policy":"Each fisher may catch up to 3kg per trip, must keep at least 1kg of their catch, and the community’s total monthly catch cannot exceed 20% of the lake’s current stock.","revised_operationalization":"At the beginning of each month the community holds a secret ballot among all fishers to elect a community monitor. The elected monitor is recorded on the community board and signs their own catch log. They are responsible for tallying cumulative catches, redistributing any excess, and enforcing the ban on over‑catchers for the following week. The monitor serves for one month only; after that a new monitor is elected by the same voting process. If a fisher feels the monitor is biased, a new vote can be held to appoint a different monitor for the next month.","reasoning":"I chose a rotating, community‑voted monitor to keep the process transparent and prevent power concentration. A secret ballot ensures privacy, while a simple majority keeps it easy. The monitor still fish and sign their catch, keeping accountability. The one‑month term and ability to replace the monitor ensure fairness and adaptability. The policy itself remains unchanged because it already balances individual needs and community sustainability."}

