{"answer":"We first look at the monthly log. At the end of each day we add up everyone’s ‘Total catch’ to get the month’s cumulative. If that sum is more than 25 % of the lake’s stock at the month’s start, we know we’ve exceeded. We then go back through the log in the order the trips were taken and subtract each fisher’s catch from the excess until the excess is gone. The fisher whose catch brings us over the limit is the one who must surrender the difference. That surrender is recorded as a negative entry in his or her ‘Personal keep’ for that day, and the fish are returned to the community pool for the next month’s quota. If more than one fisher’s catch is needed to clear the excess, each of them surrenders the amount that takes us back under the cap.

The process relies on the daily ‘Total catch’ and ‘Personal keep’ fields, and the steward checks that every fisher keeps at least 1 kg. Any fisher that keeps less than that gets flagged and banned for a month, and any excess that pushes us over the monthly cap is returned by the fisher who first caused the exceedance.
","revised_policy":"Each fisher may catch up to 4 kg per trip, must keep at least 1 kg of their catch for themselves, and the community’s total monthly catch cannot exceed 25 % of the lake’s current stock at the start of the month.","revised_operationalization":"At the end of each month the steward sums the ‘Total catch’ from all logs. If the sum exceeds 25 % of the lake’s stock at the start of the month, the steward identifies the trip(s) that caused the exceedance by iterating through the daily logs in order. The fisher whose catch pushes the total over the limit must surrender the amount needed to bring the cumulative total back to 25 % (or less) by reducing his/her ‘Personal keep’ for that day and returning the fish to the community pool. All other fishers keep their usual 1 kg personal reserve.","reasoning":"I kept the policy unchanged because the question was only about operational details. I clarified how the excess is determined and who surrenders it, staying true to the original rule."}

